##### MF1: GENERAL INFORMATION                                    2928 1451  202
                                                                  2928 1451  203
- MT451    : Descriptive data and directory                       2928 1451  204
                                                                  2928 1451  205
This text and the full directory of used MF/MT sections.          2928 1451  206
                                                                  2928 1451  207
##### MF2: RESONANCE PARAMETERS                                   2928 1451  208
                                                                  2928 1451  209
- MT151    : Resonance parameters                                 2928 1451  210
                                                                  2928 1451  211
Details about the origin of the resonance parameters for this     2928 1451  212
nuclide are given above in this description. In general, both a   2928 1451  213
Resolved and an Unresolved Resonance Range are given.             2928 1451  214
                                                                  2928 1451  215
##### MF3: REACTION CROSS SECTIONS                                2928 1451  216
                                                                  2928 1451  217
All the data present in the following MT-sections have been       2928 1451  218
calculated with TALYS, possibly normalized to experimental data.  2928 1451  219
If the maximal cross section in an excitation function over the   2928 1451  220
whole energy range does not exceed 1.e-9 b, the MT-number is not  2928 1451  221
included. Cross sections lower than 1.e-20 b are assumed to have  2928 1451  222
no physical meaning and are set to zero. All MT numbers extend up 2928 1451  223
to the highest incident energy.                                   2928 1451  224
The following reaction channels/MT numbers are generally included:2928 1451  225
                                                                  2928 1451  226
- MT1      : Total cross section                                  2928 1451  227
                                                                  2928 1451  228
Calculated with the optical model over the whole energy range,    2928 1451  229
or partly reproduced using experimental data.                     2928 1451  230
                                                                  2928 1451  231
- MT2      : Elastic scattering cross section                     2928 1451  232
                                                                  2928 1451  233
Obtained by subtracting the non-elastic cross section from the    2928 1451  234
total cross section.                                              2928 1451  235
                                                                  2928 1451  236
- MT3      : Non-elastic cross section                            2928 1451  237
                                                                  2928 1451  238
Calculated with the optical model over the whole energy range.    2928 1451  239
                                                                  2928 1451  240
- MT4      : Total inelastic cross section                        2928 1451  241
                                                                  2928 1451  242
Sum of MT=51-91.                                                  2928 1451  243
                                                                  2928 1451  244
- MT5      : (n,anything) cross section                           2928 1451  245
                                                                  2928 1451  246
MT5 contains the reactions which can not be stored in any other   2928 1451  247
MT-number. The information of MF3/MT5 can be combined with        2928 1451  248
MF6/MT5 to obtain residual production cross sections particle     2928 1451  249
production cross sections and (double-)differential cross         2928 1451  250
sections.                                                         2928 1451  251
                                                                  2928 1451  252
- MT16     : (n,2n) cross section                                 2928 1451  253
- MT17     : (n,3n) cross section                                 2928 1451  254
- MT22     : (n,na) cross section                                 2928 1451  255
- MT28     : (n,np) cross section                                 2928 1451  256
- MT51-90  : (n,n') cross section for 1st-40th excited state      2928 1451  257
- MT91     : (n,n') continuum cross section                       2928 1451  258
- MT102    : (n,gamma) cross section                              2928 1451  259
- MT103    : (n,p) cross section                                  2928 1451  260
- MT104    : (n,d) cross section                                  2928 1451  261
- MT105    : (n,t) cross section                                  2928 1451  262
- MT106    : (n,h) cross section                                  2928 1451  263
- MT107    : (n,a) cross section                                  2928 1451  264
- MT600-610: (n,p) cross section for 0th-10th excited state       2928 1451  265
- MT649    : (n,p) continuum cross section                        2928 1451  266
- MT650-655: (n,d) cross section for 0th-5th excited state        2928 1451  267
- MT699    : (n,d) continuum cross section                        2928 1451  268
- MT700-705: (n,t) cross section for 0th-5th excited state        2928 1451  269
- MT749    : (n,t) continuum cross section                        2928 1451  270
- MT750-755: (n,h) cross section for 0th-5th excited state        2928 1451  271
- MT799    : (n,h) continuum cross section                        2928 1451  272
- MT800-810: (n,a) cross section for 0th-10th excited state       2928 1451  273
- MT849    : (n,a) continuum cross section                        2928 1451  274
                                                                  2928 1451  275
##### MF4: ANGULAR DISTRIBUTIONS OF SECONDARY PARTICLES           2928 1451  276
                                                                  2928 1451  277
We use MF4 for the neutron scattering angular distribution for    2928 1451  278
discrete levels.                                                  2928 1451  279
                                                                  2928 1451  280
- MT2      : Elastic angular distribution                         2928 1451  281
                                                                  2928 1451  282
The flag LTT=3 is used to indicate a switch at some high energy,  2928 1451  283
generally 50 MeV, from a Legendre representation to a tabulated   2928 1451  284
representation. For incident energies below this energy, the      2928 1451  285
Legendre coefficients are given on a sufficiently precise energy  2928 1451  286
grid. They are a sum of calculated Legendre coefficients for      2928 1451  287
compound nucleus and shape-elastic scattering. For higher         2928 1451  288
incident energies, relative angular distributions are tabulated   2928 1451  289
on an angular grid.                                               2928 1451  290
                                                                  2928 1451  291
- MT51-70  : (n,n') angular distribution for 1st-20th state       2928 1451  292
- MT600-610: (n,p) angular distribution for 0th-10th state        2928 1451  293
- MT650-655: (n,d) angular distribution for 0th-5th state         2928 1451  294
- MT700-705: (n,t) angular distribution for 0th-5th state         2928 1451  295
- MT750-755: (n,h) angular distribution for 0th-5th state         2928 1451  296
- MT800-810: (n,a) angular distribution for 0th-10th state        2928 1451  297
                                                                  2928 1451  298
##### MF6: PRODUCT ENERGY-ANGLE DISTRIBUTIONS                     2928 1451  299
                                                                  2928 1451  300
In MF6 we store all secondary energy, angle, and energy-angle     2928 1451  301
distributions, as well as all residual and discrete + continuum   2928 1451  302
photon production cross sections for channels other than capture  2928 1451  303
(MT102) and reactions to discrete levels (MT51,etc.).             2928 1451  304
All data are generated with TALYS.                                2928 1451  305
                                                                  2928 1451  306
- MT5      : (n,anything) yields and energy-angle distributions   2928 1451  307
                                                                  2928 1451  308
MT5 contains, in this order,                                      2928 1451  309
For neutrons up to alpha particles and photons:                   2928 1451  310
- the production yields per particle for the whole energy range   2928 1451  311
- the secondary energy-angle distributions per particles which are2928 1451  312
  not represented by explicit MT numbers. On a secondary energy   2928 1451  313
  grid the relative emission spectra are given together with the  2928 1451  314
  parameters for the Kalbach systematics for angular              2928 1451  315
  distributions. Inelastic scattering cross sections for discrete 2928 1451  316
  states have been broadened and added to the continuum spectra.  2928 1451  317
For each residual product:                                        2928 1451  318
- the production yields for the whole energy range.               2928 1451  319
- the recoil                                                      2928 1451  320
These distributions can be multiplied with MF3/MT5 to get         2928 1451  321
the production cross sections and (double-)differential cross     2928 1451  322
sections.                                                         2928 1451  323
                                                                  2928 1451  324
- MT16     : (n,2n) energy-angle distr. and photon production     2928 1451  325
                                                                  2928 1451  326
First, for each type of outgoing particle, the (trivial) integer  2928 1451  327
particle yields are given. Next, on a sufficiently dense incident 2928 1451  328
energy grid the secondary energy-angle distributions are          2928 1451  329
specified by means of the relative emission spectra and the       2928 1451  330
parameters for the Kalbach systematics for angular distributions. 2928 1451  331
Next, the photon yield is tabulated as a function of incident     2928 1451  332
energy. For each incident energy, the photon production is given  2928 1451  333
for all discrete gamma lines present in the final nucleus. A      2928 1451  334
continuum photon distribution is added to this. We assume         2928 1451  335
isotropy for all produced gamma rays.                             2928 1451  336
                                                                  2928 1451  337
For the following MT-numbers, the same procedure as for MT16 is   2928 1451  338
followed:                                                         2928 1451  339
                                                                  2928 1451  340
- MT17 : (n,3n) energy-angle distr. and photon production         2928 1451  341
- MT22 : (n,na) energy-angle distr. and photon production         2928 1451  342
- MT28 : (n,np) energy-angle distr. and photon production         2928 1451  343
- MT91 : (n,n') continuum energy-angle distr. and phot. prod.     2928 1451  344
- MT649: (n,p) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     2928 1451  345
- MT699: (n,d) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     2928 1451  346
- MT749: (n,t) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     2928 1451  347
- MT809: (n,h) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     2928 1451  348
- MT849: (n,a) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     2928 1451  349
                                                                  2928 1451  350
##### MF8: RADIOACTIVE DECAY DATA                                 2928 1451  351
                                                                  2928 1451  352
For reactions to isomers, MF8 designates where the information    2928 1451  353
for isomeric versus ground state production, or residual          2928 1451  354
production can be found, i.e. for each MT number it points to     2928 1451  355
either MF6, MF9 or MF10.                                          2928 1451  356
                                                                  2928 1451  357
##### MF9: MULTIPLICITIES FOR PRODUCTION OF RADIOACTIVE NUCLIDES  2928 1451  358
                                                                  2928 1451  359
MF9 is used for isomer production by non-threshold reactions.     2928 1451  360
Final states with a lifetime that exceeds 1 second are treated    2928 1451  361
as isomer.                                                        2928 1451  362
                                                                  2928 1451  363
##### MF10: CROSS SECTIONS FOR PRODUCTION OF RADIOACTIVE NUCLIDES 2928 1451  364
                                                                  2928 1451  365
MF10 is used for isomer production by threshold reactions.        2928 1451  366
Final states with a lifetime that exceeds 1 second are treated    2928 1451  367
as isomer.                                                        2928 1451  368
                                                                  2928 1451  369
##### MF12: PHOTON PRODUCTION YIELD DATA                          2928 1451  370
                                                                  2928 1451  371
MF12 is used for the photon production for two types of reactions:2928 1451  372
- Reactions to discrete levels (MT51, etc.) For this transition   2928 1451  373
  probabilities are used (LO=2)                                   2928 1451  374
- Photons from neutron capture (MT102). Here multiplicities are   2928 1451  375
  given                                                           2928 1451  376
                                                                  2928 1451  377
##### MF14: PHOTON ANGULAR DISTRIBUTIONS                          2928 1451  378
                                                                  2928 1451  379
All photon production is considered as isotropic                  2928 1451  380
                                                                  2928 1451  381
##### MF15: PHOTON ENERGY SPECTRA                                 2928 1451  382
                                                                  2928 1451  383
For capture (MT102), the photon energy spectrum is given.         2928 1451  384
                                                                  2928 1451  385
##### MF32: COVARIANCES OF RESONANCE PARAMETERS                   2928 1451  386
                                                                  2928 1451  387
In MF32, we store the covariances of resonance parameters,        2928 1451  388
and more details are given above in this description. In most     2928 1451  389
versions of TENDL, this information is now stored in MF33.        2928 1451  390
                                                                  2928 1451  391
##### MF33: COVARIANCES OF NEUTRON CROSS SECTIONS                 2928 1451  392
                                                                  2928 1451  393
In MF33, we store the covariances of most, but not all, reaction  2928 1451  394
channels that are given in MF3. All covariances are generated     2928 1451  395
with the TASMAN code [kon19], which generates TALYS runs using    2928 1451  396
a Monte Carlo parameter method.                                   2928 1451  397
For each MT-number, the information consists of:                  2928 1451  398
1. Covariances for MTa, Ea and MTa, Ea (variances)                2928 1451  399
2. Covariances for MTa, Ea and MTa, Eb (correlations for the      2928 1451  400
   same reaction channel between different energies)              2928 1451  401
3. Covariances for MTa, Ea and MTb, Ea (correlations between      2928 1451  402
   different reaction channels for the same energy)               2928 1451  403
4. Covariances for MTa, Ea and MTb, Eb (correlations between      2928 1451  404
   different reaction channels for different energies)            2928 1451  405
Classes 1 and 2 are covered by NI-type subsubsections with        2928 1451  406
flag LB=5, followed by a LB=8 section which contains              2928 1451  407
the cross section variances multiplied by 1.e-3. Next, classes 3  2928 1451  408
and 4 are covered by flag LB=6 to account for the different       2928 1451  409
energy grids that two correlating MT-sections may have. Since     2928 1451  410
the total correlation matrix is fully symmetric, we only need to  2928 1451  411
include the correlation elements between different MT-numbers     2928 1451  412
once, and we do that in the subsection with the lowest of the     2928 1451  413
two MT-numbers. We include covariance information for all the     2928 1451  414
total cross sections, the lumped partial cross sections and the   2928 1451  415
individual inelastic cross sections per level (MT51,...). We do   2928 1451  416
not include covariances for MT600 and higher MT-numbers. For the  2928 1451  417
most important channels, we include cross-channel correlations.   2928 1451  418
                                                                  2928 1451  419
##### MF34: COVARIANCES OF ANGULAR DISTRIBUTIONS                  2928 1451  420
                                                                  2928 1451  421
In MF34, we store the covariances of the elastic scattering       2928 1451  422
angular distributions that are given in MF4/MT2. All covariances  2928 1451  423
are generated with the TASMAN code [kon19], which generates       2928 1451  424
TALYS runs using a Monte Carlo parameter method. The tables are   2928 1451  425
restricted to MT2 and contain covariances for the first 6         2928 1451  426
Legendre coefficients. Both intra- and inter-correlations of the  2928 1451  427
various Legendre coefficients are given. As in MF33, the LB5      2928 1451  428
representation is used.                                           2928 1451  429
                                                                  2928 1451  430
##### MF40: COVARIANCES OF ISOMER PRODUCTION                      2928 1451  431
                                                                  2928 1451  432
For covariances of ground state and isomeric cross sections, the  2928 1451  433
same procedures as for MF33 are used.                             2928 1451  434
                                                                  2928 1451  435
